// 103 — Anonymous functions / lambdas — DESIGN NOTE
// `x => x * 2`, `[](int x){ return x*2; }`, `lambda x: x*2`. Concise
// when you have a one-line callback and don't want to declare a
// whole top-level function for it. Costs: closures need to capture
// surrounding state (allocation), lambdas need a calling convention
// for "function value" (often a fat pointer), and stack traces print
// `<lambda>` at the use site instead of a name you can search for.
//
// AiLang doesn't ship lambdas. Every callable is a top-level
// `Function` declaration with a name. The "extra typing" buys: every
// callable is searchable by name (grep / relmem just work), no
// closure-capture allocation surprises, no anonymous frames in
// crash traces.
//
// When the callback is genuinely one-shot, name it once and use it
// once — see 117_nested_functions.ailang for the convention.
Function.Double {
    Input: x: Integer
    Output: Integer
    Body: {
        ReturnValue(Multiply(x, 2))
    }
}

SubRoutine.Main {
    // Where another language might write `[1,2,3].map(x => x*2)`,
    // AiLang spells it out: a named function and an explicit loop.
    PrintMessage("Double(7) = ")
    PrintNumber(Double(7))
    PrintMessage("\nDouble(13) = ")
    PrintNumber(Double(13))
    PrintMessage("\n")
}
RunTask(Main)
